AI Transformation Playbook
    1. Execute pilot projects to gain momentum
    2. Build an in-house AI team
    3. Provide broad AI training
    4. Develop and AI strategy
    5. Develop internal and external communications

1. Execute pilot projects to gain momentum
    - More important for the initial project to succeed rather than be the most valuable
    - Show traction within 6-12 months
    - Can be in-house or outsourced
2. Build an in-house AI team
    - Build a centralized AI Team seperate from other Business Units (BU)
        - Matrix them into other BUs to support their work
    - Build company wide platform for all BUs
    - AI function can be under CTO, CIO, CDO, etc. or a new CAIO
    - To get the AI team started, the company or CEO should provide funding to build AI unit
        - Instead of requiring the AI unit to get funding from the Business Units (BU)
3. Provide broad AI training
    - Role and what they should learn 
    (Hours is not a good metric to show learning/Capabilities)
        a. Executives and senior business leaders (about 4 hrs.)
            - What AI can do for your enterprise
            - AI strategy
            - Resource allocation
        b. Leaders of divisions working on AI projects (12 hrs.)
            - Set project direction (Technical and Business diligence)
            - Resource allocation
            - Monitor Progress
        c. AI engineer trainees (at least 100 hrs.)
            - Build and ship AI software
            - Gather Data
            - Execute on specific AI projects
The smart CLO knows they sould curate rather than create content for employee training
